# ── 22. Off the Glass's opponent still plays like a person (added 2026-10-18) ───────────────────────────────────────────────
# Why: Amir's racket in assets/js/rally.js used to chase the ball's current spot at a speed that rose and fell with the score, and a bot
# that simply followed the ball won 58 points out of 58. He now sees the ball late, walks to where a shot will cross his end, aims with
# an error and mistimes one now and then, and his form moves a step between points: a dozen numbers (the AMIR block) that lean on each
# other, so moving one quietly makes him unbeatable or a doormat and nothing errors. scripts/test_rally_sim.js loads the real rally.js
# in Node (no browser, about 4 s) and plays four bots with a person's limits against it, 9,000 s of game time each: the point share of
# a sharp, an average and a tired bot must stay in order and in range (the average one near 58%), the one that never errs must still
# lose some points, the ball must stay on the court, his form must change only between points and really matter, and his top speed
# must be one number whoever leads. To retune: change a number in the AMIR block, run the test, read the table it prints.
if git diff --cached --name-only --diff-filter=ACM | grep -qE '^(assets/js/rally\.js|scripts/test_rally_sim\.js)$'; then
